From e970b6135a63d68125b53d6cc5e532917722af70 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Angelo Arifi <arifilabs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 17:22:33 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] arifi-sync: root-fix the destructive series regen, and
 mechanize protected-win preservation

Two chokepoint changes and the operator procedure that drives them. Both come out of the
independent r2 check on lane-209 rank 3.

1. `series regen` was DESTRUCTIVE-THEN-ABORT.

   It deleted every file under `patches/series/` and only then called `format_patch()`, which is
   where the linearity pre-flight STOP lives. On a branch that fails that pre-flight - which
   `arifi/main` does today, with 10 pre-existing merge commits - it exited rc=2 having left the
   repository with no series at all. Measured 2026-09-04: 415 tracked `.patch` files deleted by
   what its operator ran as a read-only probe. Nothing was staged or committed and `git restore`
   recovered it, but a destructive-then-abort chokepoint that depends on the operator noticing is
   not a safe chokepoint. UPDATE-RUNBOOK 4.1 could only warn about it; this fixes it.

   The order is now pre-flight -> generate into a temp directory -> build SERIES and MANIFEST.md
   from that temp directory -> swap. The first statement that touches `patches/series/` is the
   swap, so any failure above it leaves the committed series byte-for-byte intact. A second
   pre-flight is added: regen_preflight() refuses a range containing a commit with no provenance,
   because such a commit produces a MANIFEST row asserting an attribution the repository does not
   have.

2. Protected wins are now a machine-readable register with a fail-closed guard.

   President mandate 2026-09-04, verbatim: "anything from upstream of llama.cpp or the forks we
   ingest, must not overwrite what we have, we must compare and make sure we keep the winning
   formula, maybe something upstream from anyone might be better than ours but we must
   confirm/test".

   tools/arifi-sync/protected-wins.json records seven banked wins, each with its mechanism,
   commit, protected paths, semantic anchors, evidence, quality gate, workload, measured effect
   and valid conditions. `protected-win validate` fails on an incomplete active entry AND on an
   entry whose path or anchor has stopped existing - the state in which a guard passes while
   defending nothing. `protected-win check --incoming <ref>` refuses when an incoming change
   touches a protected path or deletes a line carrying a protected anchor, unless a resolution
   naming three arms, a quality result and resolvable evidence covers that exact pair. `bump` runs
   it at leg 1b/4 and refuses on a non-zero result; there is no skip flag.

   The manifest is AUTHORED, not generated: protected paths, anchors, quality gates and workloads
   are not derivable from a trailer, and a fabricated field would make the guard lie. What IS
   derivable is the candidate set, and `protected-win discover` prints every commit with a real
   Measured-effect that no entry covers - so a landed win cannot quietly stay unregistered while
   no field is ever invented to close a row.

3. UPDATE-RUNBOOK gains 4.1c, the weakest-successor procedure verbatim with exact commands and
   paths, and the 4.1 hazard note is rewritten to describe the prevention rather than only the
   recovery. Sections 3 and 4.1 now call the preflight by name, because the manual TAKE path never
   reaches `bump`.

Verified by python tools/arifi-sync/test_arifi_sync.py - 14 cases, all green. Each builds a
throwaway git repository, so nothing depends on this repository own history, which matters because
arifi/main cannot exercise the regen success path at all. The two regen negative cases assert that
patches/series/ is byte-for-byte unchanged after a refusal, by content hash and not by file count:
a count would have passed the old code on the day it deleted 415 files.

Origin: ArifiLabs (native)
Lane: lane-209
Measured-effect: no engine behaviour changed and no shipped default moved; tooling and
  documentation only, verified by the 14-case suite including four fail-closed negative cases.
